# ── Secrets — NEVER commit these ──
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
server/.env
server/.env.*
!server/.env.example

# ── Engineering docs — NEVER on github, in any form ──
# Both the plaintext .md AND the AES-256-GCM ciphertext (.md.enc) are
# excluded. Earlier policy committed the encrypted blobs on the theory
# that they were meaningless without ENGINEERING_DOCS_KEY. We're now
# stricter: defense in depth means if the key ever leaks (env-var
# accidentally logged, dev laptop compromised, CI pipeline scraped),
# the attacker shouldn't also have a copy of the ciphertext sitting
# in a public-or-stolen git repo.
#
# Deployment workflow (no git involvement):
#   1. Edit plaintext in docs/private/*.md on the dev machine.
#   2. Run `npm run docs:encrypt` to produce *.md.enc next to each.
#   3. Upload the *.md.enc files to the server out-of-band:
#        railway run -- bash -c 'cat > server/docs/private/X.md.enc' < X.md.enc
#        (or rsync over SSH, or whatever the deploy target supports)
#   4. Server reads from PRIVATE_DOCS_DIR at boot; key comes from
#      ENGINEERING_DOCS_KEY env var on the deploy target.
#
# The whole docs/private/ directory is excluded. On a fresh clone the
# directory simply won't exist; the server logs "engineering docs
# disabled — docs/private/ directory missing" and admin chat falls
# back to public docs only, which is the safe default.
docs/private/

